{
  "teamId": 108,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 Los Angeles Angels entered the season with sky-high hopes built around the otherworldly talent of Mike Trout and the unprecedented two-way phenomenon Shohei Ohtani, but the year would ultimately prove a familiar story of near-misses and missed opportunities. The Angels played winning baseball through April, fueled by unexpected contributors like Zack Cozart and Andrelton Simmons providing timely clutch hits, and finished the month at 14-11 with genuine playoff momentum.\n\nMay became the season's first major turning point — and not in a good way. The bullpen, led by the chronically unreliable Jim Johnson, became a revolving door of damage. Johnson alone surrendered multiple crushing leads in the same week, and the rotation's early promise gave way to inconsistency. The Angels finished May at 14-15, slipping below .500, and the pattern of winning games only to have the bullpen surrender them in late innings would define the summer months.\n\nJune and July offered flashes of brilliance — Kole Calhoun delivered clutch home runs, Ian Kinsler sparked rallies, and Martin Maldonado provided unlikely big moments behind the plate — but the Angels could never string together the sustained run they needed. Blake Parker and Justin Anderson took turns as the bullpen's most unreliable arms, surrendering leads in high-leverage situations time and again. The team finished July at 11-13, slipping further from the wild card picture.\n\nAugust and September told the story of a team that fought hard but simply lacked the depth to compete night after night. A DJ LeMahieu grand slam off Jim Johnson in late August encapsulated the season — talent undermined by execution at the worst moments. The Angels finished 80-82, two games under .500, as a team that scored 721 runs and allowed 722, living and dying on the thinnest of margins and never quite finding the consistency to push over the top despite boasting arguably the sport's best player and one of its most captivating newcomers.",
